{"name":"Fired Clay","hex":"#884444","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fired","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["dismissed, terminated from employment.","Heated in a furnace, kiln, etc., to become permanently hardened."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of fire"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fired"},{"term":"clay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral substance made up of small crystals of silica and alumina, that is ductile when moist; the material of pre-fired ceramics.","An earth material with ductile qualities."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add clay to or to spread clay onto.","To purify using clay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fire clay","blocks":[{"text":"Fire clay is a range of refractory clays used in the manufacture of ceramics, especially fire brick. The United States Environmental Protection Agency defines fire clay very generally as a \"mineral aggregate composed of hydrous silicates of aluminium (Al2O3·2SiO2·2H2O) with or without free silica.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Fornello_inv.3916_IF_77656.jpg/330px-Fornello_inv.3916_IF_77656.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_clay"},{"title":"Clay","blocks":[{"text":"Clay is a type of fine-grained natural soil material containing clay minerals. Most pure clay minerals are white or light-coloured, but natural clays show a variety of colours from impurities, such as a reddish or brownish colour from small amounts of iron oxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg/330px-Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/